1. Update Ubuntu
sudo apt update
sudo apt upgrade -y

Install required packages:
sudo apt install -y curl wget unzip gnupg apt-transport-https

2. Install Loki
Create Loki user:
sudo useradd --system --no-create-home --shell /bin/false loki

Create directories:
sudo mkdir -p /etc/loki
sudo mkdir -p /var/lib/loki
sudo mkdir -p /var/lib/loki/chunks
sudo mkdir -p /var/lib/loki/compactor

Download Loki:
cd /tmp
wget https://github.com/grafana/loki/releases/latest/download/loki-linux-amd64.zip

Extract:
unzip loki-linux-amd64.zip

Install:
chmod +x loki-linux-amd64
sudo mv loki-linux-amd64 /usr/local/bin/loki

Verify:
loki --version

3. Configure Loki
sudo nano /etc/loki/loki-config.yaml


Paste below text:

auth_enabled: false

server:
  http_listen_port: 3100
  grpc_listen_port: 9096

common:
  path_prefix: /var/lib/loki
  replication_factor: 1

  ring:
    kvstore:
      store: inmemory

schema_config:
  configs:
    - from: 2024-01-01
      store: tsdb
      object_store: filesystem
      schema: v13

      index:
        prefix: index_
        period: 24h

storage_config:
  filesystem:
    directory: /var/lib/loki/chunks

limits_config:
  allow_structured_metadata: true
  volume_enabled: true

compactor:
  working_directory: /var/lib/loki/compactor



Set permissions:
sudo chown -R loki:loki /etc/loki
sudo chown -R loki:loki /var/lib/loki


4. Create Loki service
sudo nano /etc/systemd/system/loki.service


Paste:

[Unit]
Description=Loki Log Aggregator
Wants=network-online.target
After=network-online.target

[Service]
User=loki
Group=loki
Type=simple

ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/loki \
  -config.file=/etc/loki/loki-config.yaml

Restart=on-failure

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target




Start Loki:
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl enable --now loki
sudo systemctl status loki

Test:
curl http://localhost:3100/ready
Expected:
ready



5. Install Grafana Alloy

Add Grafana repository:


sudo mkdir -p /etc/apt/keyrings

wget -q -O - https://apt.grafana.com/gpg.key | \
  gpg --dearmor | \
  sudo tee /etc/apt/keyrings/grafana.gpg > /dev/null

echo "deb [signed-by=/etc/apt/keyrings/grafana.gpg] https://apt.grafana.com stable main" | \
  sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/grafana.list


Update:
sudo apt update


Install Alloy:
sudo apt install -y alloy

Verify:
alloy --version


6. Configure Alloy

Backup existing configuration:

sudo cp /etc/alloy/config.alloy /etc/alloy/config.alloy.bak

Open configuration:
sudo nano /etc/alloy/config.alloy

Use:


local.file_match "varlogs" {
  path_targets = [
    {
      __path__ = "/var/log/*.log",
      job      = "varlogs",
    },
  ]
}

loki.source.file "varlogs" {
  targets    = local.file_match.varlogs.targets
  forward_to = [loki.write.local.receiver]
}

loki.write "local" {
  endpoint {
    url = "http://localhost:3100/loki/api/v1/push"
  }
}



Save the file.

Format it:
sudo alloy fmt /etc/alloy/config.alloy

Restart Alloy:
sudo systemctl restart alloy
sudo systemctl enable alloy
sudo systemctl status alloy



7. Give Alloy access to logs
Add Alloy to the adm group:

sudo usermod -aG adm alloy

Restart:
sudo systemctl restart alloy


Check:
id alloy
You should see adm in the groups.


8. Check Alloy logs

sudo journalctl -u alloy -f

Look for errors.
You can also check Alloy's HTTP endpoint:
curl http://localhost:12345/-/ready



clear


9.Install Grafana

Install prerequisites:

sudo apt-get install -y apt-transport-https wget gnupg

Create keyring:
sudo mkdir -p /etc/apt/keyrings

Download Grafana GPG key:
sudo wget -O /etc/apt/keyrings/grafana.asc https://apt.grafana.com/gpg-full.key

Set permissions:
sudo chmod 644 /etc/apt/keyrings/grafana.asc

Add repository:
echo "deb [signed-by=/etc/apt/keyrings/grafana.asc] https://apt.grafana.com stable main" | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/grafana.list

Update:
sudo apt-get update

Install Grafana OSS:
sudo apt-get install -y grafana

Step 8 — Start Grafana
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl enable grafana-server
sudo systemctl start grafana-server
sudo systemctl status grafana-server










11. Add Loki to Grafana
In Grafana:
Connections → Data sources → Add data source


Select:
Loki


For URL enter:
http://localhost:3100

Click:
Save & test

You should get a successful connection.



12. View logs

Go to:
Explore

Select:
Loki



All Recent Logs
Query:
{job="varlogs"}


Run:
{job="varlogs"}
You should see your Ubuntu logs.


For errors:
{job="varlogs"} |= "error"


For failed messages:
{job="varlogs"} |= "failed"


13. Generate a test log


Run:


echo "Loki test log from Ubuntu" | sudo tee -a /var/log/loki-test.log


Then in Grafana Explore:
{job="varlogs"} |= "Loki test"



create your Loki Log Monitoring Dashboard from this screen.

Step 1 — Create dashboard
Click the blue + Create dashboard button in the middle.
Then select Add visualization.

Step 2 — Select Loki
Grafana will ask you to select a data source.

Choose:
Loki
You should then see the panel editor.

Step 3 — Create your first panel: Total Logs

In the query area, enter:
sum(count_over_time({job="varlogs"}[$__interval]))
On the right side, change the visualization to:
Stat

Set the panel title to:
Total Logs
Then click Apply.

Step 4 — Add Error Logs panel
Click:
Add → Visualization

Select Loki and use:
sum(count_over_time({job="varlogs"} |= "error" [$__interval]))
Visualization:

Stat
Title:
Errors
Click Apply.

Step 5 — Add Warning Logs
Add another visualization:
sum(count_over_time({job="varlogs"} |= "warn" [$__interval]))
Visualization:
Stat
Title:
Warnings

Step 6 — Add Log Volume
Add another visualization.

Query:
sum(count_over_time({job="varlogs"}[$__interval]))
Choose:
Time series
Title:
Log Volume
This will show how the number of logs changes over time.


Step 7 — Add Recent Logs
Add another visualization.
Query:
{job="varlogs"}
Choose:
Logs
Title:
Recent Logs

This is the important panel where you'll see the actual Ubuntu log messages.

